From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brennoflavio <contato@brennoflavio.com.br>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 20:47:38 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] webapi: mock Chrome data-channel offers and host ICE natively

Expose Window-only RTCPeerConnection, RTCDataChannel,
RTCSessionDescription, RTCIceCandidate and RTCPeerConnectionIceEvent with
native prototypes, promises, configuration, event handlers and state.
Generate coherent local data-channel SDP, per-peer opaque mDNS host
candidates and asynchronous gathering completion. Honor relay-only policy
without manufacturing a relay candidate. Do not inspect host interfaces,
contact STUN/TURN or implement UDP, DTLS, SCTP or media transport. A peer
never reports connected and a data channel never reports open.

Support implicit/repeated local offers, candidate-enriched local description
round-trips, rollback, restart and close. Keep offer history separate from
live channels. Own callback, channel, event-candidate and event-target edges
in the JS heap, with temporary roots only for queued tasks. Release closed
channels and removed/once/aborted listeners; detach peers during frame and
context teardown. Resolve no remote negotiation: remote/media operations
are unsupported rather than reporting fictitious success. Arbitrary or
modified local SDP, certificates, media tracks and the full RTCStatsReport
surface remain outside this mock.

Follow the WebRTC IDL/state model and reference Chrome 154's standard
constructor, descriptor, configuration and return-type behavior; retain the
fork's existing Chrome 155/macOS MacBook Neo persona. Reference:
https://w3c.github.io/webrtc-pc/

Use temporary ignored diagnostics, not new upstream unit tests. Leave the
benchmark and all earlier patches unchanged. The intended benchmark gain
is webrtc-gathers and webrtc-leak; inspector timing and ServiceWorkers are
separate work.
